Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables, columns and how they will be used  

• bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_trips  
  – start_station_id  : id of the station where a trip begins – used to join with the station‑dimension table  
  – end_station_id   : id of the station where a trip ends – used to join with the station‑dimension table  
  – trip_id       : can be counted to represent “one trip”  

• bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations  
  – station_id     : primary key, used to join with the trips table  
  – council_district  : numeric council‑district code we have to aggregate on  
  – status       : tells us whether the station is active; keep only status = 'active'  

Logical conditions that will be applied  

1. Keep only stations whose status = 'active'.  
2. Keep only trips whose start_station_id ≠ end_station_id (trip ends at a different station).  
3. Join trips to the stations table twice (start and end) and keep only the rows where the two joined council_district values are equal (trip starts and ends inside the same district).  
4. Count trips per council_district and pick the one with the highest count.  

Below are ten exploratory → incremental → final BigQuery SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows unless otherwise noted) that lead up to the answer.

```sql
-- 1. Look at the different station statuses
SELECT DISTINCT status
FROM `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect active stations with their council districts
SELECT station_id, name, council_district, status
FROM `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations`
WHERE status = 'active'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Get a feel for trips that start and finish at different stations
SELECT trip_id, start_station_id, end_station_id
FROM `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_trips`
WHERE start_station_id <> CAST(end_station_id AS INT64)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Count total trips (any status) per station pair
SELECT start_station_id, end_station_id, COUNT(*) AS trips
FROM `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_trips`
WHERE start_station_id <> CAST(end_station_id AS INT64)
GROUP BY start_station_id, end_station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Join start station info (showing council district) – sample
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.start_station_id,
       ss.council_district AS start_cd
FROM `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_trips`  AS t
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations` AS ss
  ON t.start_station_id = ss.station_id
WHERE ss.status = 'active'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Join both start & end stations, keep only active ones – sample
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.start_station_id, ss.council_district AS start_cd,
       t.end_station_id,   es.council_district AS end_cd
FROM `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_trips`  AS t
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations` AS ss
  ON t.start_station_id = ss.station_id
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations` AS es
  ON CAST(t.end_station_id AS INT64) = es.station_id
WHERE ss.status = 'active'
  AND es.status = 'active'
  AND t.start_station_id <> CAST(t.end_station_id AS INT64)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Filter to trips whose start & end districts are the same
SELECT t.trip_id,
       ss.council_district AS district
FROM `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_trips`  AS t
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations` AS ss
  ON t.start_station_id = ss.station_id
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations` AS es
  ON CAST(t.end_station_id AS INT64) = es.station_id
WHERE ss.status = 'active'
  AND es.status = 'active'
  AND ss.council_district = es.council_district
  AND t.start_station_id <> CAST(t.end_station_id AS INT64)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Count trips per council district (still limited)
SELECT ss.council_district AS district,
       COUNT(*) AS trips_same_cd_diff_station
FROM `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_trips`  AS t
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations` AS ss
  ON t.start_station_id = ss.station_id
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations` AS es
  ON CAST(t.end_station_id AS INT64) = es.station_id
WHERE ss.status = 'active'
  AND es.status = 'active'
  AND ss.council_district = es.council_district
  AND t.start_station_id <> CAST(t.end_station_id AS INT64)
GROUP BY district
ORDER BY trips_same_cd_diff_station DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Same as #8, but remove the LIMIT to see all districts
SELECT ss.council_district AS district,
       COUNT(*) AS trips_same_cd_diff_station
FROM `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_trips`  AS t
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations` AS ss
  ON t.start_station_id = ss.station_id
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations` AS es
  ON CAST(t.end_station_id AS INT64) = es.station_id
WHERE ss.status = 'active'
  AND es.status = 'active'
  AND ss.council_district = es.council_district
  AND t.start_station_id <> CAST(t.end_station_id AS INT64)
GROUP BY district
ORDER BY trips_same_cd_diff_station DESC;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: return only the numeric council district with the highest count
SELECT district
FROM (
  SELECT ss.council_district AS district,
         COUNT(*) AS trips_same_cd_diff_station
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_trips`  AS t
  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations` AS ss
    ON t.start_station_id = ss.station_id
  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.austin_bikeshare.bikeshare_stations` AS es
    ON CAST(t.end_station_id AS INT64) = es.station_id
  WHERE ss.status = 'active'
    AND es.status = 'active'
    AND ss.council_district = es.council_district
    AND t.start_station_id <> CAST(t.end_station_id AS INT64)
  GROUP BY district
  ORDER BY trips_same_cd_diff_station DESC
  LIMIT 1
);
```

